>
  <p>
    Good Google marketing for a home service company is four things working as one system: a
    correctly categorised Business Profile, a website that converts, Local Services Ads for cheap
    proof, then Search ads for scale. Run properly, that spine took
    <a href="/case-studies/honest-hanks">Honest Hank’s from $25k to $80k months in eight months</a>
    and <a href="/case-studies/rewired">Rewired from $60k to $220k+ over three years</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    “Do Google” is not a strategy. This is what the actual work looks like, and what it produces.
  </p>

  <h2>The Google Business Profile does the heaviest lifting</h2>
  <p>
    It’s free, it sits above the organic results, and for most local trades it drives more calls
    than the website. What matters on it is unglamorous: the correct primary category, a complete
    services list, a real address where you can show one, consistent name and phone details matching
    your site, and reviews that keep arriving.
  </p>
  <p>
    The category is the one people get wrong, and it’s catastrophic when they do. Motha Electric’s
    profile said “electrical installation service” instead of “electrician,” which meant Google
    quietly declined to show them for the term everybody searches. Correcting it produced one to
    three calls a day within about a week ,
    <a href="/blog/google-category-relevance">the full story is here</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The website's job is conversion, not decoration</h2>
  <p>
    Keyword-led pages that turn map and ad traffic into phone calls. If your organic visitors aren’t
    booking, ads won’t rescue you, you’ll just pay more per person who doesn’t call.
  </p>
  <p>
    The structure that works answers a homeowner’s questions in the order they arrive: can I trust
    you, do you do my job, do you come to my area, what happens if I call, what happens if I don’t.
    <a href="/case-studies/dry-duck">Dry Duck</a> was built on exactly that skeleton from a roughly
    $500 brief and was booking estimates within weeks of launch, before ads did anything at all.
    Details on <a href="/services/websites">how we build them</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Local Services Ads first, Search ads second</h2>
  <p>
    LSAs are usually the right first paid lane. You pay per lead instead of per click, the Google
    Guaranteed badge borrows trust a newer brand hasn’t earned yet, and the placement sits at the
    very top of the page.
  </p>
  <div class="receipt">
    <div class="receipt-label">WHAT LSA-LED LOOKS LIKE AT SCALE</div>
    <p>
      Hooked Up Electric has run 100 to 200 Google leads a month for over two years on an LSA-led
      setup, expanding from one county to four. In Jason’s words, the company grew 600% in 2024.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    <a href="/services/paid-ads">Search ads</a> come after, when you need volume LSAs can’t supply,
    or higher-ticket work, and you can afford a cost per acquisition that’s honestly higher. Running
    Search first is how owners end up convinced Google doesn’t work for their trade.
  </p>

  <h2>What does "good" actually look like in numbers?</h2>
  <p>
    Three things, in this order of importance:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Revenue climbing faster than Google spend</strong>, measured at the business level.
      Not per-channel attribution, which is largely fiction for local trades.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Booked estimates on the calendar</strong>, jobs a human has qualified, rather than a
      CRM full of form fills an agency calls leads.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Map-pack presence in the neighbourhoods you want</strong>, not a citywide average that
      hides the fact you’re invisible in the wealthy suburb.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Our four case studies entered from completely different places, scale what you have, grow the
    crew, start from nothing, expand the territory, and the Google spine underneath was the same
    every time. That’s the actual argument for fundamentals over tactics.
  </p>

  <h2>What Google can't do for you</h2>
  <p>
    It won’t fix a sales process where nobody calls back within the hour. It won’t make a brand
    nobody trusts trustworthy. It won’t conjure a truck roster you don’t have, Rewired’s jump to
    $220k months needed twelve crew, not twelve more clicks.
  </p>
  <p>
    It also won’t invent high-ticket jobs if the account is optimised for the cheapest possible
    click, and it won’t stay stable on its own while competitors keep earning reviews and you don’t.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    Good Google marketing is disciplined fundamentals, honest measurement, and the guts to turn
    spend down when you’re full and up when you’re ready.
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    None of that is a growth hack. It’s the boring version, and it’s the one that compounds for
    three years.
  </p>
